  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nRemaining showers and embedded thunderstorms across the southeast\npart of the forecast area will continue to decrease in intensity and\nmove out of the area. Rain rates decreased substantially which\nallowed for the flood watch to be cancelled since there would be no\nmore new flooding. But flooding from previous rain will persist.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nCold front located near the northern edge of the forecast area at the\nbeginning of the period will only make slow progress this morning \nbut then push south this afternoon into the evening. It will become \nmoderately unstable ahead of the front, but it is not clear whether \nthere will be convection in the warm sector. If some storms can \ndevelop, there there is the potential for some strong to damaging \nwind gusts. \n\nA short wave will move across the region tonight inducing a wave of \nlow pressure along the boundary. A low level jet will accompany this.\nPrecipitable water will remain high and the warm cloud layer will\nstill be deep. So as more showers and thunderstorms develop north of\nthe front, there will be the potential for heavy rain once again.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 3)\nCooler airmass will settle across the region. A deep trough \napproaching from the east will close off as it moves across the area \nmidweek. At the surface, an inverted trough will extend along the\nwestern slopes of the Appalachians. This combination will result in\nshowery conditions, particularly across eastern counties.",
